WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.130
I asked our agent to remind me to tidy

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.130
the library tomorrow at nine.

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.420
It proposed a reminder to

4
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.660
tisums the library.

5
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:12.330
Tisums!

6
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.750
If nobody had checked,

7
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.470
that’s what I’d have got.

8
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.570
Today: checking an agent’s

9
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.750
work before it counts.

10
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.610
An agent’s answer is the

11
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.530
last thing you see.

12
00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.270
By then, the tool has already run.

13
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.890
The moment to check is earlier:

14
00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.710
after the model writes a call, and

15
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.230
before the app runs it.

16
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.640
Check three things in every call.

17
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.370
Is it the right tool?

18
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.700
Did every name and number come from

19
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.800
your words, or from a result?

20
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.910
And does each detail have the right

21
00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.550
shape, like a time that exists?

22
00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.790
Darsi Tiny’s real slips look like this.

23
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.740
Asked about class nine Z, which doesn’t

24
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.200
exist, it called the timetable for nine C.

25
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.110
It copied a time of

26
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.930
twenty-five o’clock as it was.

27
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.970
And tidy became tisums.

28
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.850
A checker reviews each

29
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.030
call before it runs.

30
00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.310
It doesn’t need to be clever: a few

31
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.130
strict rules in code catch a model’s

32
00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.310
slips without guessing.

33
00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:21.770
What the rules can’t

34
00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:22.970
decide goes to a person.

35
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.680
The team lab’s checker has five rules.

36
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.260
The tool must be switched on.

37
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.680
Every detail must have the right shape.

38
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.780
Every name and number must come

39
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.880
from your words or a result.

40
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.720
No step before it may have failed.

41
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.010
And any action needs a person’s yes.

42
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:45.790
Quick check!

43
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.430
I asked about class nine Z, and the agent

44
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.550
proposes the timetable for nine C.

45
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.480
Which rule stops it?

46
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.490
Nine C isn’t in your

47
00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.530
request, or in any result.

48
00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.850
So the checker stops the call before

49
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.270
anything runs, and hands it back to you.

50
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.250
Then you ask again, in other words.

51
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.560
Keep a record, too.

52
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.980
A good agent app logs every call:

53
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.920
what was asked, what ran, what was

54
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.980
stopped, and who said yes.

55
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.850
When a parent asks why a message

56
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.830
went out, the log answers.

57
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.510
In the app, Darsi Tiny is the

58
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.490
planner and proposes each call.

59
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.430
Send the weather request:

60
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.250
the checker passes it.

61
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.430
Send the library reminder and nine

62
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.510
Z: the checker stops them.

63
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.420
Then switch the checker off.

64
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:41.460
Your turn.

65
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.380
The checker passes a reminder

66
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.820
for tomorrow at eight.

67
00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.190
Who should decide whether it’s set?

68
00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.840
A person, because a reminder is an action.

69
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.080
Remember: check the call before it runs.

70
00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.600
A few strict rules catch a model’s slips.

71
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.120
And a person gives the final

72
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.980
yes, with a record kept.

73
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.720
Try it in class tomorrow:

74
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.740
play Be the checker.

75
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.300
Give groups proposed calls on

76
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.660
cards, some with planted slips.

77
00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.710
Groups apply the five rules and

78
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.030
stop or pass each card.

79
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.450
Which slip was hardest to spot?
